The project I worked on is called **Captivated**. It is a **multi-tenant SaaS customer communication platform**.

The main purpose of the platform is to help businesses communicate with their customers from a single application. It provides a unified inbox where agents can manage customer conversations across different communication channels such as **SMS, calls, chat, email, Facebook Messenger, and RCS**.

For example, suppose a customer sends an SMS to a business. The message comes through a communication provider such as Twilio or another provider and reaches our **Ruby on Rails API**.

Our Rails application identifies the organization and the customer, finds or creates the appropriate conversation, processes the message, and stores it in **PostgreSQL**.

We also have a separate **Node.js and Socket.IO service** for real-time communication. Once a new message or conversation update is available, Socket.IO pushes that event to the connected agent's browser, so the agent can see the message immediately without refreshing the page.

The platform is multi-tenant, so multiple businesses can use the same application while their users, contacts, conversations, and messages remain isolated from each other.

From the backend side, I worked mainly with **Ruby on Rails, REST APIs, PostgreSQL, Redis, Sidekiq, Docker, Doorkeeper, Sorcery, Pundit, and Active Storage**.

In short, Captivated helps businesses **manage customer communication and conversations from one centralized platform**, while our Rails API handles the core business logic and our Node.js Socket.IO service handles real-time updates.

